<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yorkshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yorkshire is a historic county of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the largest in the country, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. It formed the core of the Anglian kingdom of Deira and later of the Viking Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, whose settlers left hundreds of Scandinavian place-names. Its traditional divisions were the North, East and West Ridings around the city of York.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yorkshire.jpg" alt="Yorkshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kreuzschnabel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2015_Swaledale_from_Kisdon_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yorkshire takes its name from the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, being York's shire. The name York is believed to come from the Brittonic Eburākon, probably meaning place of yew trees, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Eorfowīc to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and Jórvík to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>. The south-west includes Leeds, Sheffield, Bradford, Doncaster and Wakefield, and Hull lies in the south-east. The North York Moors fill the north-east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-york">Vale of York</a> the centre, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a>, including the Yorkshire Dales, the west. The historic boundaries were reasonably stable from 1182, when western areas went to the new county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, until 1889.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the area was held by British Celtic tribes, the Brigantes in the north and west and the Parisi in what is now the East Riding. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> left, small kingdoms arose, among them Deira in the east, Ebrauc around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and Elmet in the west. In 1066, shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold II</a> marched north and defeated Tostig and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> at Stamford Bridge, where both were killed. Many castles, among them Richmond, Pickering, Skipton and York, were built in the Norman period.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, between the houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and Lancaster, battles were fought at Wakefield and Towton, the latter called the bloodiest ever fought on English soil. In the 16th and 17th centuries the wool textile industry moved from cottage work in the old market towns to mills in the West Riding powered by Pennine streams, helping Wakefield, Halifax, Leeds and Huddersfield to grow, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining first came into prominence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The county was divided into three ridings, East, West and North, a word of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> origin from Threthingr, a third. Each was divided into wapentakes. York became a county corporate in 1396 and Hull in 1440, independent of the ridings. Each riding had its own lord-lieutenant from 1660, and from 1889 to 1974 the ridings were administrative counties.</p><p>Yorkshire's culture draws on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a>, Norse Vikings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, and its dialect has roots in Old English and Old Norse. The white rose, once the badge of the Plantagenet House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, is its emblem, and Yorkshire Day is observed on 1 August. York Minster is the largest Gothic cathedral in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yorkshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Wilberforce, Member of Parliament for <b>Yorkshire</b>, carried the cause into the House of Commons, which voted down his motion in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1811 and 1816 the Luddites of Nottinghamshire, <b>Yorkshire</b> and Lancashire smashed machinery, and in 1812 Parliament made the breaking of stocking frames punishable by death.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Yorkshire</b> croppers broke the cropping machines made by Enoch Taylor of Marsden with a hammer they called “Enoch”, purportedly shouting, “Enoch made them, and Enoch shall break them.”</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Where it was composed is also disputed: F. W. Moorman proposed <b>Yorkshire</b> and E. Talbot Donaldson West Mercia.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Its enemies took it up too: at the Battle of Famars in 1793, the British 14th Regiment of Foot attacked the French to its tune, and the regiment was later awarded it as a battle honour and quick march, a tradition the <b>Yorkshire</b> Regiment keeps.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li><li><blockquote>Further variants are called &quot;We've Been a While-A-Wandering&quot; and &quot;<b>Yorkshire</b> Wassail Song&quot;, and satirical ones were used in the 1987 television special A Claymation Christmas Celebration.</blockquote><a href="/songs/here-we-come-a-wassailing">Here We Come A-wassailing</a></li></ul></section>
